%I A400964 #6 Oct 15 2026 20:17:33
%S A400964 1,2,2,4,4,6,6,8,6,8,10,12,12,12,13,13,16,13,18,16,16,16,22,20
%N A400964 Maximum number of points that can be chosen from the n X n discrete torus Z_n X Z_n so that no three of them lie on a line and no two lie in the same row or column.
%C A400964 A line of Z_n X Z_n is a set {(a + w*t1, b + w*t2): w in Z_n} with gcd(t1, t2, n) = 1, as in A400437. Equivalently, a(n) is the largest cap of Z_n X Z_n contained in the graph of a permutation of Z_n.
%C A400964 a(n) <= min(n, A400437(n)). a(n) = n for n = 1, 2, 4, 6, 8, 12 and for no other n <= 24.
%C A400964 a(p) >= p - 1 for odd primes p, attained by the hyperbola {(x, 1/x): x != 0}; equality holds for p <= 23.
%C A400964 Terms a(1)-a(20) and a(23) are from Kurz. a(21) = a(22) = 16 and a(24) = 20 were found by SAT search with cube-and-conquer (see linked file); Kurz gives 16-17, 16-17 and 20-22.
%C A400964 Kurz gives 19 <= a(25) <= 22; the linked file proves a(25) <= 19. Kurz also gives 18 <= a(26) <= 24, 18 <= a(27) <= 25, 22 <= a(28) <= 27, a(29) = 28, 22 <= a(30) <= 29.
%H A400964 Jim Fowler, Andrew Groot, Deven Pandya and Bart Snapp, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1203.6604">The no-three-in-line problem on a torus</a>, arXiv:1203.6604 [math.CO], 2012.
%H A400964 Sascha Kurz, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.4333">Caps in Z_n^2</a>, Serdica J. Computing 3 (2009), 159-178; arXiv:1401.4333 [math.CO], 2014.
%H A400964 Azmi C. Özgen, <a href="/A400964/a400964.txt">Witnesses for n = 1..24, SAT proofs of a(21), a(22), a(24) and a(25) <= 19, and a checker</a>
%e A400964 a(4) = 4: {(0,0), (1,2), (2,3), (3,1)} has one point in each row and column and no three points on a line of Z_4 X Z_4.
%Y A400964 Cf. A000769, A400437.
%K A400964 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A400964 1,2
